From 8c82c5a1672970e62ef0dd071a60b5afd262dd89 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Micheal Waltz <micheal@ecliptik.com>
Date: Thu, 3 Sep 2026 22:22:29 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] dos: disable the shared backend's silence-detect throttle for
 Passage's  quiet passages

Round 2 on the 486DX2-50 + Mach64 rig, with patches 0039/0040 in, put
the port back to pacer-bound in every ordinary window of a life: work
38-41 ms, real sleep of 19-21 ms, 3-4 frames over budget per 300, no
resyncs. Three windows per life were the exception -- the first ~20 s
after each of the song's loop points (the song is 136 s long) -- and
they were the whole remaining gap between a 304 s life and the 298.3 s
a 15 fps life takes: engine pump 8.8-9.8 ms per frame from ~260 calls
per 300 frames, a frame summing to 70-75 ms, 67-77 frames over budget
and 4-7 debt-clamp resyncs in each such window.

The mechanism is the Sound Blaster backend's Shape A post-mix silence
detect (doskutsu SDL/0054): when a mixed chunk is all-silence it skips
the ring write and sleeps 10 ms as a throttle. Right for a game whose
audio is effects over silence; wrong for Passage, where the quiet start
of the song is content. With nothing written the ring fill never rises,
so the engine's ring-depth pump (playGame's pump loop, target 150 ms)
calls again every frame, and every one of those calls -- plus the
cooperative audio-thread iterate that the pacer's SDL_Delay(0) yield
runs when a frame is behind -- pays the 10 ms sleep. The same
three-window pump burst was visible in this port's DOSBox-X smokes
before Round 1 (windows 0, 6 and 13, ~250 calls each) and was read at
the time only as "something at the song loop".

The backend has a killswitch (SDL/0056): its hint read is
SDL_GetHint("SDL_HINT_DOS_SILENCE_DETECT"), strict "0" disables,
resolved once at OpenDevice. This sets it with override priority next
to the port's other DOS hints, before SDL_Init. No shared-layer change.
Silent chunks are then written to the ring like any other; the backend's
"always fill" path is its own older default.

Verified (DOSBox-X, correctness only): a full natural life on the
shipping build ran clean (14.97 fps, fps_p50 15.00 / fps_p95 14.98), and
the diagnostic twin's STAGEDBG.LOG shows pump_avg 0.00 and zero pump
calls in every one of its 15 windows, including windows 0, 6 and 13,
where the same diagnostic without this hint recorded 8.3-8.7 ms per
frame from ~250 calls per window; its CMOS RTC witness read 299 s
against 299 s of engine time. Real-hardware confirmation is the
Round 3 cells.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_017TKEXpxndNddTzMQkSdtBX
---
 gameSource/game.cpp | 27 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 27 insertions(+)

diff --git a/gameSource/game.cpp b/gameSource/game.cpp
index ed59d03..eb3835a 100644
--- a/gameSource/game.cpp
+++ b/gameSource/game.cpp
@@ -732,6 +732,33 @@ int mainFunction( int inArgCount, char **inArgs ) {
                               SDL_HINT_OVERRIDE );
     SDL_SetHintWithPriority( SDL_HINT_DOS_MAX_BPP, "16",
                               SDL_HINT_OVERRIDE );
+
+    // DOS-PORT: turn off the shared Sound Blaster backend's post-mix
+    // silence-detect throttle (doskutsu's SDL/0054 "Shape A", killswitch
+    // SDL/0056). When a mixed chunk is all-silence that path skips the
+    // ring write and sleeps 10 ms. That is the right trade for a game
+    // whose audio is sound effects over silence; for Passage it is the
+    // opposite: the song has genuinely quiet passages (the first ~20 s
+    // after each 136 s loop), and silence there is content that must
+    // reach the ring like any other chunk. With the write skipped the
+    // ring fill never rises, so the engine's own ring-depth pump (see
+    // playGame) calls again every frame and every call -- and every
+    // cooperative audio-thread iterate inside the pacer's yield -- pays
+    // that 10 ms. Measured on the 486DX2-50 + Mach64 rig (Round 2, patch
+    // 0037's diagnostic re-applied on top of 0040): in the three quiet
+    // windows of a life the pump costs 8.8-9.8 ms per frame from ~260
+    // calls per 300 frames, the frame sums to 70-75 ms against a 66.67 ms
+    // budget, 67-77 of 300 frames run over and the debt clamp resyncs 4-7
+    // times, while every other window sits at 60 ms with 15-20 ms of
+    // slack and no resyncs. Those three windows are the whole remaining
+    // gap between a 304 s life and the 298.3 s a 15 fps life takes. The
+    // same three-window pump burst reproduces under DOSBox-X. Set with
+    // override priority so a stray environment variable cannot re-enable
+    // it; the backend reads the value once at OpenDevice. Name is the
+    // backend's own string (it is read via SDL_GetHint, not a public
+    // SDL_HINT_* macro).
+    SDL_SetHintWithPriority( "SDL_HINT_DOS_SILENCE_DETECT", "0",
+                              SDL_HINT_OVERRIDE );
 #endif
 
     // DOS-PORT: SDL3 removed SDL_INIT_NOPARACHUTE (no signal-handler
